"A service dependency map shows observed or declared runtime calls between services for a specified environment and observation window.\n\nUse when: Investigate downstream dependencies and failure propagation. Compare intended service relationships with trace-derived behavior.\n\nAvoid when: A complete ordered transaction or static source import graph is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote services, with environment and ownership in compact annotations. Directed edges identify callers and callees. Optional edge measurements carry units, time windows, and an observed or estimated label.\n\nBuild: Choose an environment, window, and source such as distributed traces. Normalize service identities and disclose missing instrumentation. Draw callers toward dependencies, grouping external services separately. Place edge measurements in clear corridors and suppress low-value links only with a documented rule.\n\nCheck: An absent edge is not claimed to prove no dependency. Traffic direction and aggregation rules are explicit. All numbers have provenance; unknown values remain unknown. Dense hubs are split into readable views before connectors intersect labels.\n\nPitfalls: Presenting a sampled trace map as an exhaustive architecture. Using edge thickness for traffic without a legend or units.\n\nExample: Production traces show Checkout calling Pricing and Payment during the documented observation interval.\n\nAlternatives: System landscape diagram, Sequence diagram."
